        "original_id": "3c9c2c16-22be-54ad-bf7e-fa7180cc642d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Works: Iphigénie en Tauride, Der Barbier von Bagdad, Orfeo ed Euridice\n- People: Christoph Willibald Gluck, Peter Cornelius, Nicolas-François Guillard, Ranieri de' Calzabigi\n- Concepts: opera, libretto, One Thousand and One Nights, Orpheus myth\n\nFacts:\n- In 1779, Iphigénie en Tauride (\"Iphigenia in Tauris\") was composed by Christoph Willibald Gluck as an opera in four acts.\n- Nicolas-François Guillard’s most famous work was the libretto for Iphigénie en Tauride, which was his first libretto and was set by Christoph Willibald Gluck after Gluck had initially rejected it.\n- Orfeo ed Euridice, also known in French as Orphée et Eurydice, is an opera by Christoph Willibald Gluck based on the myth of Orpheus and set to a libretto by Ranieri de' Calzabigi.\n- Der Barbier von Bagdad (\"The Barber of Baghdad\") is a comic opera in two acts by Peter Cornelius with a German libretto by the composer.\n- Der Barbier von Bagdad is based on \"The Tale of the Tailor\" and \"The Barber’s Stories of his Six Brothers\" from One Thousand and One Nights.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
